1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,200
 Hey everyone, Ryan here and welcome back to our operative dentistry series.

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:13,120
 This video will be all about tooth colored restorations, both composite resin and glass

3
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:14,120
 ionomer.

4
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:19,880
 We're going to cover a lot of high yield facts in this video and we'll start with composite.

5
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:25,680
 Now composite resin has the added benefit of retention via bonding, so let's kick off

6
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:31,240
 this video talking about what adhesive dentistry is all about.

7
00:00:31,240 --> 00:00:37,760
 So adhesive dentistry was originally devised by Michael Buonacor in 1955.

8
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:45,160
 He was inspired by the industrial use of phosphoric acid to adhere paints and resins to metallic

9
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:50,320
 surfaces, so he asked why not try this for teeth.

10
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:58,680
 So acid etching transforms a low energy smooth enamel surface into a high energy irregular

11
00:00:58,680 --> 00:01:05,080
 surface, thereby increasing its surface free energy and wet ability.

12
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:11,120
 What that basically means is a fluid based resin will easily spread over the surface,

13
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:15,840
 whereas on this one it would just bead up and fall right off.

14
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:23,680
 The resin is also able to flow into these irregularities and when it polymerizes or solidifies,

15
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:30,200
 the material becomes mechanically interlocked within that enamel surface.

16
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:34,760
 Which explains why this bond is so strong.

17
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:41,640
 As long as the surface is contaminated with saliva beforehand, you can get over 20 megapascals

18
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:48,720
 of sheer bond strength, which is insanely strong for such a small surface area.

19
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:54,680
 However, bonding to dentin is much more difficult.

20
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:59,400
 If you do get a good bond, it can be just as strong as the enamel bond, but the problem

21
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:06,040
 is it's not nearly as reliable or predictable as it is when bonding to enamel.

22
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:07,520
 So why is that?

23
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:11,480
 Well there are four main reasons I want to share with you that are frequently tested

24
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:13,240
 on the board exam.

25
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:15,560
 Number one is composition.

26
00:02:15,560 --> 00:02:23,000
 So the enamel is about 90% mineral, which we know from our first video is hydroxyapatite,

27
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,320
 and dentin has much more organic matter like type 1 collagen and water and much less mineral

28
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:32,400
 in comparison.

29
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:39,400
 So dentin is not as impacted by the effects of the acid, so that's reason number one.

30
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:41,160
 Reason number two is structure.

31
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:47,640
 The enamel prisms are much more regular and etchable, while the dentinol tubules and collagen

32
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:52,720
 are like amaze, one of my mentors described it as a bowl of spaghetti, which I think is

33
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:55,720
 a really helpful analogy.

34
00:02:55,720 --> 00:03:01,800
 The third reason is depth, so the fluid filled dentinol tubules are much larger and more

35
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:05,120
 numerous as you get closer to the pulp.

36
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:10,700
 The dentinol fluid interferes with our bond integrity, so there's less bond strength

37
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:13,360
 the deeper you go into the tooth.

38
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:19,360
 The same can also be said for sclerotic dentin, which is often deeper into the tooth as well,

39
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:22,000
 and it's much more acid resistant.

40
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:27,040
 And lastly we have the smear layer, and I like to think of it as the sawdust from cutting

41
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:32,920
 with the burr, it's kind of like shavings of hydroxyapatite and collagen, that plug

42
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:40,040
 the orifices of the dentinol tubules, and these smear plugs decrease dentin permeability

43
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:42,440
 by nearly 90%.

44
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:50,080
 So for all of these reasons, dentin is much harder to predictably bond to than enamel.

45
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:54,640
 Okay so now that we know what's going on with both enamel and dentin, let's talk through

46
00:03:54,640 --> 00:04:01,440
 the actual clinical steps and materials, and the first step is the acid etch.

47
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:08,880
 So we want to place around 37% phosphoric acid gel etchant on the desired area and leave

48
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:14,840
 it on for 10 to 30 seconds, routinely 15 seconds is the magic number.

49
00:04:14,840 --> 00:04:20,960
 Now etch does a couple things, one of the most important is it cleans surface debris

50
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:27,280
 and removes the smear layer on both enamel and dentin, so it dissolves away that pesky

51
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:30,600
 sawdust that's interfering with our bond.

52
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:36,240
 For enamel, we're looking for a chalky or frosty white appearance, and that's an optical

53
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:44,160
 effect, the enamel temporarily loses its translucency due to the formation of micro porosities,

54
00:04:44,160 --> 00:04:51,560
 and that's what we saw on that first slide, changing from a smooth surface to a high-energy

55
00:04:51,560 --> 00:04:56,040
 irregular surface, and that's what's causing that frosty white appearance.

56
00:04:56,040 --> 00:05:03,380
 And for the dentin, the etch exposes a layer of collagen and opens and widens those dentinol

57
00:05:03,380 --> 00:05:05,200
 tubules.

58
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:12,040
 Then we rinse the etch off with water for about 10 seconds, and the important thing here

59
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:18,140
 is we can gently air dry that water to attain that frosty white appearance, but we want

60
00:05:18,140 --> 00:05:21,720
 to make it like freshly drying beach sand.

61
00:05:21,720 --> 00:05:28,240
 So after this stage, we don't want the tooth to be soaking wet like the ocean, but we also

62
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:31,800
 don't want it to be bone dry like the hot sand over here.

63
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:39,480
 We want it somewhere in the middle, some kind of moist middle ground between soaking wet

64
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:40,960
 and bone dry.

65
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:47,200
 Alternatively, you can use a desensitizer like gluma to re-wet the tooth after you dry

66
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:48,560
 the water off.

67
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:53,840
 All right, so after etching, the second step is the primer.

68
00:05:53,840 --> 00:06:00,440
 The primer is typically hema, which is hydroxyethyl methacrylate, and that's what that stands

69
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:08,080
 for, and hema consists of an amphiphilic resin monomer wedding agent.

70
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:14,920
 Hydrophobic means it has a hydrophobic as well as a hydrophilic end.

71
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:19,680
 The hydrophilic end is what will contact the tooth surface.

72
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:29,000
 And then acetone, ethanol, or water act as the solvent that carries this valuable monomer.

73
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:35,160
 An excellent, excellent board's question is that hema, if it contacts skin, can cause an

74
00:06:35,160 --> 00:06:41,680
 allergic reaction known as contact dermatitis, and that comes up all the time.

75
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:47,000
 The primer does not open dental tubules, that's what the etch did.

76
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:52,760
 It instead infiltrates the tooth surface and wets the surface of the dentin to prevent

77
00:06:52,760 --> 00:06:59,280
 the collagen from collapsing, and thus allows for better retention.

78
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:05,560
 Or I should say better adhesion, which is where the next step of the process comes in.

79
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:11,320
 So the third and final step before actually restoring the tooth is the bond, sometimes

80
00:07:11,320 --> 00:07:14,960
 also called the sealer, or the adhesive.

81
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:23,120
 So the adhesive contains bis-GMA, which stands for bis-phenol-A, glycidal methacrylate, which

82
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:24,920
 is quite a mouthful.

83
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:31,560
 So it's good practice to gently air-dry both the primer and the bond after application

84
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:37,720
 for just a few seconds in order to evaporate the solvent, which we no longer need, leaving

85
00:07:37,720 --> 00:07:41,880
 behind the valuable monomers.

86
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:46,480
 Hema, in this case, bis-GMA, in this case.

87
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:51,720
 And it leaves those monomers in a nicely thinned layer inside that cavity prep, and then we

88
00:07:51,720 --> 00:07:56,760
 cure both the prime and the bond with a curing light.

89
00:07:56,760 --> 00:07:58,440
 So what does the bond or adhesive do?

90
00:07:58,440 --> 00:08:05,200
 Well, it chemically bonds to the underlying primer and eventually the overlying composite

91
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:08,440
 resin through methyl methacrylate bonds.

92
00:08:08,440 --> 00:08:13,440
 Let's take a look at what that looks like under the microscope.

93
00:08:13,440 --> 00:08:19,520
 So all of these components working in tandem create what's called the hybrid layer, which

94
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:24,000
 is the key to great bond strength.

95
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:29,600
 So remember, we have inter-prismatic enamel that has dissolved away from the etch, leaving

96
00:08:29,600 --> 00:08:37,560
 behind in a regular, high energy surface, and we have patent-dental tubules with collagen

97
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:41,440
 fibers that hopefully have not collapsed on each other.

98
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:53,120
 So we're looking at this ideal surface for enamel, this ideal surface for dentin.

99
00:08:53,120 --> 00:09:02,040
 And so imagine those surfaces that were into those openings were flowing primer and adhesive,

100
00:09:02,040 --> 00:09:06,480
 which together form resin tags.

101
00:09:06,480 --> 00:09:12,760
 So those are these extensions into those openings, in this case we're looking at dentin.

102
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:20,160
 So this is extension of that primer into those patent-dental tubules.

103
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:23,220
 And this is where the magic happens.

104
00:09:23,220 --> 00:09:30,200
 So the adhesive resins lock into both the micro-prostities of etch enamel and the inter-tubular

105
00:09:30,200 --> 00:09:39,440
 dentin, and then we cure them in place so they are solidified and physically locked in there.

106
00:09:39,440 --> 00:09:45,920
 So the key to adhesive dentistry is this micro-mechanical bond.

107
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:51,560
 Now there are chemical bonds like we discussed in the previous slide between the primer and

108
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:57,000
 the adhesive, and between the adhesive and the overlying composite, but the key between

109
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:07,560
 the tooth and everything else is this micro-mechanical retention offered by the resin tags.

110
00:10:07,560 --> 00:10:10,360
 Pretty cool stuff.

111
00:10:10,360 --> 00:10:16,600
 So let's unpack this really helpful chart from Spirit Education in order to better understand

112
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:23,160
 the four generations of bonding systems, most commonly used today and also most commonly

113
00:10:23,160 --> 00:10:25,600
 tested on the board exam.

114
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:29,680
 This might seem super overwhelming, but we're going to go through it step by step, and I

115
00:10:29,680 --> 00:10:32,680
 promise you you'll understand all of this.

116
00:10:32,680 --> 00:10:38,320
 So we're going to start with the left side of the chart first, with the total etch otherwise

117
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:41,520
 known as etch and rinse systems.

118
00:10:41,520 --> 00:10:47,480
 These are where you have the acid etch in a separate syringe, like the one I've been

119
00:10:47,480 --> 00:10:50,200
 showing you throughout this video.

120
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:55,520
 These systems require that the dentin be left moist like we talked about with the beach

121
00:10:55,520 --> 00:10:56,680
 example.

122
00:10:56,680 --> 00:11:01,800
 We want the dentin to be kept slightly wet after rinsing off the etch, otherwise if it's

123
00:11:01,800 --> 00:11:07,520
 dried out completely, those collagen fibrils will collapse on each other.

124
00:11:07,520 --> 00:11:15,280
 This column all the way to the left is our fourth generation, and this is considered

125
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:17,000
 the gold standard.

126
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:24,240
 The etch is its own syringe, the primer is in its own bottle, and the bond or adhesive

127
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:29,920
 is also in its own bottle, so it's a multi-bottle three-step process.

128
00:11:29,920 --> 00:11:33,920
 Optibond FL is an example of that.

129
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:37,640
 The next column over is the fifth generation.

130
00:11:37,640 --> 00:11:44,120
 The etch is still in its own syringe, but the prime and bond are combined into one bottle,

131
00:11:44,120 --> 00:11:50,640
 so this becomes a two-step process, and so theoretically there's less room for error.

132
00:11:50,640 --> 00:11:57,720
 Prime and bond universal and Optibond solo plus are examples of this one.

133
00:11:57,720 --> 00:12:03,640
 There's also a technique known as selective etch, and that's essentially these same two

134
00:12:03,640 --> 00:12:11,560
 generations, except the etchant is applied selectively to enamel only, and this may reduce

135
00:12:11,560 --> 00:12:16,800
 post-op sensitivity while maintaining the strong bond to enamel.

136
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:22,400
 Alright, now let's move to the right side of the chart, which are the self-etch systems,

137
00:12:22,400 --> 00:12:26,400
 where the etchant is now included in the bottle.

138
00:12:26,400 --> 00:12:30,080
 It's no longer in its own separate syringe.

139
00:12:30,080 --> 00:12:36,400
 The big thing with self-etches is that they leave behind the smear layer, because the

140
00:12:36,400 --> 00:12:41,760
 acid etch is less potent, so there are some considerations here.

141
00:12:41,760 --> 00:12:46,760
 There's less post-op sensitivity because it's a weaker etch, and the sawdust is left behind

142
00:12:46,760 --> 00:12:55,040
 to block and clog those dental tubules, and it's usually recommended to use a carbide

143
00:12:55,040 --> 00:13:02,400
 burr if you're using a self-etch system because the diamond leaves too much smear layer behind.

144
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:07,800
 Also to consider, the enamel bond is not as strong with the self-etch system.

145
00:13:07,800 --> 00:13:12,840
 As you can imagine, again, the etchant is just less potent.

146
00:13:12,840 --> 00:13:20,760
 Also, these materials typically need to be refrigerated, and there's no rinsing allowed

147
00:13:20,760 --> 00:13:22,280
 after the etch step.

148
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:24,720
 The etch is just left in place.

149
00:13:24,720 --> 00:13:29,560
 So in other words, it does not require that wet bonding phenomenon that we've been talking

150
00:13:29,560 --> 00:13:31,160
 about.

151
00:13:31,160 --> 00:13:36,540
 So let's move to the sixth generation, which combines the etch and the primer in one bottle

152
00:13:36,540 --> 00:13:41,640
 this time, and the bond is in its own second bottle.

153
00:13:41,640 --> 00:13:46,240
 So it's still a two-step process like the fifth generation, but it's just a little bit

154
00:13:46,240 --> 00:13:49,160
 different in how the components are combined.

155
00:13:49,160 --> 00:13:57,440
 Clear fill SE is an example of the sixth generation, SE, of course, standing for self-etch.

156
00:13:57,440 --> 00:14:04,240
 And then lastly, we have our seventh generation, which combines the etch, primer, and the adhesive

157
00:14:04,240 --> 00:14:06,080
 all into one little package.

158
00:14:06,080 --> 00:14:10,760
 So this is a true all-in-one system with only one step.

159
00:14:10,760 --> 00:14:18,200
 The ELPOP is an example of this seventh generation bonding system, and it's frequently used in

160
00:14:18,200 --> 00:14:20,240
 orthodontics.

161
00:14:20,240 --> 00:14:28,300
 All right, so now we can finally talk about composite resin, which was developed by Ray

162
00:14:28,300 --> 00:14:36,480
 Bowen in the late 1950s, just a few years after acid etching by Buonacor was discovered.

163
00:14:36,480 --> 00:14:41,760
 So composite resin itself is a really interesting term.

164
00:14:41,760 --> 00:14:47,640
 So composite means that it's made from two or more constituent materials that when mixed

165
00:14:47,640 --> 00:14:55,560
 together make an entirely new material, and resin means it's a highly viscous pre-polymer

166
00:14:55,560 --> 00:15:02,680
 with reactive groups that when cured by either blue light or chemical initiation, it undergoes

167
00:15:02,680 --> 00:15:08,880
 cross-linking or polymerization from a liquid to a solid.

168
00:15:08,880 --> 00:15:14,200
 So that name composite resin has a lot of information packed into it, and honestly it

169
00:15:14,200 --> 00:15:17,440
 perfectly describes what it is.

170
00:15:17,440 --> 00:15:21,600
 So there are a couple of components that we need to talk about that are in every single

171
00:15:21,600 --> 00:15:23,640
 composite resin.

172
00:15:23,640 --> 00:15:32,920
 So first, the organic or resin matrix is usually bis-GMA, which is the same bis-phenol-A glycidal

173
00:15:32,920 --> 00:15:38,560
 methacrylate that we saw in the adhesive or the bond before.

174
00:15:38,560 --> 00:15:45,640
 Now this can also be other resins like tag-DMA, U-DMA, and others, all of which are different

175
00:15:45,640 --> 00:15:47,800
 dimethacrylates.

176
00:15:47,800 --> 00:15:53,640
 The hot-button issue with composites was concerned over cytotoxicity, and this is in relation

177
00:15:53,640 --> 00:16:02,680
 to the resin matrix, because particularly of this bis-phenol-A, which can mimic the effects

178
00:16:02,680 --> 00:16:10,080
 of estrogen, you've probably heard of BPA, which is bis-phenol-A, because it's in disposable

179
00:16:10,080 --> 00:16:15,200
 water bottles, and if you have a refillable water bottle, it might even mention that it's

180
00:16:15,200 --> 00:16:18,480
 BPA-free on the bottom somewhere.

181
00:16:18,480 --> 00:16:25,160
 Now leaching of BPA can occur through where of the composite, or with resin that's left

182
00:16:25,160 --> 00:16:26,160
 uncured.

183
00:16:26,160 --> 00:16:32,200
 However, its potential to elicit adverse health effects is pretty negligible, because so little

184
00:16:32,200 --> 00:16:35,960
 BPA is released in this way.

185
00:16:35,960 --> 00:16:42,880
 Next we have the filler particles, which are these light green chunks swimming within this

186
00:16:42,880 --> 00:16:46,640
 light blue matrix.

187
00:16:46,640 --> 00:16:52,000
 So they are powdered glass, or powdered ceramic glass.

188
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:58,400
 Silica is synonymous with silicon dioxide, which is found in quartz.

189
00:16:58,400 --> 00:17:04,940
 So the filler particles specifically can be barium silicate, strontium silicate, zinc,

190
00:17:04,940 --> 00:17:07,320
 or even silver silicate.

191
00:17:07,320 --> 00:17:12,080
 And the filler particles range in size and shape, which affects various properties of

192
00:17:12,080 --> 00:17:15,240
 the composite, which we'll go over in the next slide.

193
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:21,040
 The filler also is the most radio-pake element in the composite, which is a desirable feature,

194
00:17:21,040 --> 00:17:24,400
 so it shows up well on radiographs.

195
00:17:24,400 --> 00:17:30,640
 And lastly, you may have noticed the darker green coating on each of these filler particles,

196
00:17:30,640 --> 00:17:35,120
 and this refers to the coupling agent, which is silane.

197
00:17:35,120 --> 00:17:40,440
 So the filler particles within the resin composites are coated with silane in order to promote

198
00:17:40,440 --> 00:17:44,760
 adhesion to the matrix, and basically glue everything together.

199
00:17:44,760 --> 00:17:50,120
 And if I'm being honest, guys, this entire slide should be in red text.

200
00:17:50,120 --> 00:17:55,280
 It's so important, but I wanted everything to color coordinate with the image over here.

201
00:17:55,280 --> 00:18:02,600
 But everything on the slide is incredibly high-yield board exam info.

202
00:18:02,600 --> 00:18:08,080
 So there are many different categories of composites based on what they're made out of.

203
00:18:08,080 --> 00:18:12,560
 So we'll go through each of these so you understand what they all mean.

204
00:18:12,560 --> 00:18:22,000
 So macrophil or conventional composites are 80% filler.

205
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:28,040
 They have an 8 micron particle size.

206
00:18:28,040 --> 00:18:34,240
 And these huge filler particles make the macrophil composites nice and strong.

207
00:18:34,240 --> 00:18:41,000
 But the problem is it's really rough, and the resin matrix wears at a faster rate, increasing

208
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,440
 its roughness over time.

209
00:18:43,440 --> 00:18:48,880
 So for those reasons, the macrophil composites are no longer used, but they can be seen in

210
00:18:48,880 --> 00:18:51,520
 older patients.

211
00:18:51,520 --> 00:19:03,400
 The microphil composite is 40% filler, and it has a very small filler particle size of

212
00:19:03,400 --> 00:19:07,320
 0.04 microns.

213
00:19:07,320 --> 00:19:10,560
 So it's just the opposite to the macrophil.

214
00:19:10,560 --> 00:19:15,920
 It's weak, but it has good polish and good wear resistance, because there's more matrix

215
00:19:15,920 --> 00:19:17,880
 holding everything in place.

216
00:19:17,880 --> 00:19:23,960
 And it also flexes under pressure, so it can be good for bruxing patients.

217
00:19:23,960 --> 00:19:31,080
 The hybrid composite combines the favorable properties of the macrophil, so it has a high

218
00:19:31,080 --> 00:19:37,840
 amount of filler with the smooth surface of the microfilm, because it has smaller particle

219
00:19:37,840 --> 00:19:39,080
 size.

220
00:19:39,080 --> 00:19:41,160
 So the hybrid is going to have the best of both worlds.

221
00:19:41,160 --> 00:19:49,480
 We're going to have 80% filler, and a smaller particle size, about one micron, for each

222
00:19:49,480 --> 00:19:52,240
 filler particle.

223
00:19:52,240 --> 00:19:58,840
 But we get even more advanced with nanofill and nanohybrid, which is Filtech Supreme is

224
00:19:58,840 --> 00:20:05,160
 an example of this, and they're the most popular composite restorative materials in use today.

225
00:20:05,160 --> 00:20:16,600
 The nanofill has extremely small filler particles, we're talking 0.005 to 0.01 microns in particle

226
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:19,120
 diameter, so super small.

227
00:20:19,120 --> 00:20:25,600
 And these really small particles can agglomerate with each other to provide a full range of

228
00:20:25,600 --> 00:20:27,200
 filler sizes.

229
00:20:27,200 --> 00:20:32,280
 The nanohybrid incorporates these nanofill particles and integrates them with these

230
00:20:32,280 --> 00:20:34,720
 other sizes here.

231
00:20:34,720 --> 00:20:41,640
 So we're really just in an attempt to improve on the material with each of these steps.

232
00:20:41,640 --> 00:20:46,200
 Now these last two are kind of in their own categorization.

233
00:20:46,200 --> 00:20:51,200
 Flowable is talking about any kind of composites that have very low filler amount, so it's

234
00:20:51,200 --> 00:20:53,880
 kind of like the microfill.

235
00:20:53,880 --> 00:21:01,160
 And the good thing with the Flowable is that it has a lower wear resistance.

236
00:21:01,160 --> 00:21:05,880
 The packable is the other side of the coin, it has a high filler amount that makes it

237
00:21:05,880 --> 00:21:10,000
 really viscous and almost feels like a macrofill.

238
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:15,520
 It's supposed to handle like an amalgam based on how viscous that material is.

239
00:21:15,520 --> 00:21:20,920
 So packable and flowable are just other ways you can categorize composite types.

240
00:21:20,920 --> 00:21:27,120
 So the takeaways from this slide are that the larger fillers have more strength, but

241
00:21:27,120 --> 00:21:33,140
 they do not polish or wear as well, they're rougher because of those large fillers.

242
00:21:33,140 --> 00:21:39,480
 And then materials with higher filler content or higher filler percentage exhibit lower water

243
00:21:39,480 --> 00:21:46,440
 absorption because there's less matrix or basically less room for water to seep in between

244
00:21:46,440 --> 00:21:49,360
 those particles.

245
00:21:49,360 --> 00:21:53,720
 So that's everything you need to know for those composite types based on filler particle

246
00:21:53,720 --> 00:21:57,520
 and filler particle size.

247
00:21:57,520 --> 00:22:01,840
 Alright we also have two main categories of how a composite polymerizes.

248
00:22:01,840 --> 00:22:05,920
 So we're talking about self cure versus light cure.

249
00:22:05,920 --> 00:22:11,520
 Self cure also called chemical cure is a two paste system.

250
00:22:11,520 --> 00:22:19,520
 So one of the pastes is called the base and it contains both composite and benzoyl peroxide,

251
00:22:19,520 --> 00:22:25,160
 which is the initiator and all this stuff is really good test information and I can't

252
00:22:25,160 --> 00:22:26,160
 stress that enough.

253
00:22:26,160 --> 00:22:30,160
 So the benzoyl peroxide is the initiator.

254
00:22:30,160 --> 00:22:36,120
 Interestingly, benzoyl peroxide is also a well known acne treatment.

255
00:22:36,120 --> 00:22:42,160
 So my memory tip here is that acne cream is the initiator for healthy skin and it's also

256
00:22:42,160 --> 00:22:45,320
 the initiator for self cure composites.

257
00:22:45,320 --> 00:22:49,720
 So the other paste in this two paste system is called the catalyst and it contains both

258
00:22:49,720 --> 00:22:53,320
 composite and tertiary amines.

259
00:22:53,320 --> 00:22:57,640
 The tertiary amine is the activator.

260
00:22:57,640 --> 00:23:02,440
 So you can think of A and A for activator.

261
00:23:02,440 --> 00:23:09,520
 So these two components are equal parts of these two pastes are mixed together and then

262
00:23:09,520 --> 00:23:15,200
 the polymerization reaction begins and once this happens the operator has a limited amount

263
00:23:15,200 --> 00:23:21,000
 of working time, usually just two minutes or so before it becomes too stiff to manipulate.

264
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:24,160
 So you have to work pretty quickly.

265
00:23:24,160 --> 00:23:29,680
 The light cure composite is the most common because number one there's no mixing required

266
00:23:29,680 --> 00:23:31,760
 and it's not a race against the clock.

267
00:23:31,760 --> 00:23:37,200
 You have time to shape the composite and you decide when to polymerize it with the curing

268
00:23:37,200 --> 00:23:38,680
 light.

269
00:23:38,680 --> 00:23:44,640
 So this is a single paste system where no longer working with a base or in a catalyst.

270
00:23:44,640 --> 00:23:49,040
 Number four quinone is the photo initiator.

271
00:23:49,040 --> 00:23:51,360
 This is this comes up all the time.

272
00:23:51,360 --> 00:23:59,280
 My memory tip for this one is you like to take photos when you go camping outside, camping

273
00:23:59,280 --> 00:24:01,480
 and photos.

274
00:24:01,480 --> 00:24:11,320
 So blue visible light of 468 nanometers approximately is what initiates this free radical polymerization

275
00:24:11,320 --> 00:24:14,600
 through the action of camp four quinone.

276
00:24:14,600 --> 00:24:22,240
 There's also what's called a dual cure composite that contains elements of both of these systems.

277
00:24:22,240 --> 00:24:28,280
 So that's particularly useful for really big build ups where the light can only be used

278
00:24:28,280 --> 00:24:34,040
 to cure most of the surface composite but for areas that the light cannot reach the chemical

279
00:24:34,040 --> 00:24:41,240
 reaction will still carry out and polymerize itself.

280
00:24:41,240 --> 00:24:48,020
 So composites can shrink about two to three percent as they polymerize which is an incredibly

281
00:24:48,020 --> 00:24:49,760
 important concept.

282
00:24:49,760 --> 00:24:55,920
 Basically free radicals break one of the carbon carbon double bonds in the BIS-GMA to form

283
00:24:55,920 --> 00:25:01,680
 a single bond and another free radical undergoes the same reaction with another monomer and

284
00:25:01,680 --> 00:25:05,280
 adds it to this growing polymer chain.

285
00:25:05,280 --> 00:25:11,160
 And as it keeps recruiting monomers the volume of the resin decreases and that's how shrinkage

286
00:25:11,160 --> 00:25:12,160
 happens.

287
00:25:12,160 --> 00:25:17,240
 And eventually the side groups share electrons with each other via covalent bonds which is

288
00:25:17,240 --> 00:25:24,640
 called cross-linking resulting in stronger stiffer cured material.

289
00:25:24,640 --> 00:25:31,200
 So the configuration factor or C factor is the ratio of bound to unbound surfaces of

290
00:25:31,200 --> 00:25:32,200
 the composite.

291
00:25:32,200 --> 00:25:37,720
 So this is a little bit of a complicated topic so let's really unpack what this means.

292
00:25:37,720 --> 00:25:42,280
 And we'll start with a class one composite.

293
00:25:42,280 --> 00:25:47,600
 So we know for class one only one surface is prepped these are pit and fissure carries.

294
00:25:47,600 --> 00:25:55,720
 So if you can imagine this as a cube we have one side that is unbound just freely exposed

295
00:25:55,720 --> 00:26:01,520
 and that leaves five sides that are bound or bonded to tooth structure.

296
00:26:01,520 --> 00:26:07,040
 If we're talking about a class one posterior prep those five surfaces will be the facial,

297
00:26:07,040 --> 00:26:10,880
 single, mesial, distal, and pulpit walls.

298
00:26:10,880 --> 00:26:19,120
 So we have a ratio of five bound surfaces to one unbound surface.

299
00:26:19,120 --> 00:26:25,960
 So our C factor is simply five divided by one which is five.

300
00:26:25,960 --> 00:26:32,640
 In class two we have four bound surfaces to two unbound surfaces.

301
00:26:32,640 --> 00:26:38,600
 So our C factor is four divided by two which is two and so on.

302
00:26:38,600 --> 00:26:45,240
 If we went through each one of these GV black classes you realize there's an inverse relationship

303
00:26:45,240 --> 00:26:49,320
 between the class and the C factor.

304
00:26:49,320 --> 00:26:57,560
 So the higher our classes from one up to five the lower our C factor is from five down to

305
00:26:57,560 --> 00:27:00,440
 point two.

306
00:27:00,440 --> 00:27:03,240
 So why does all this matter?

307
00:27:03,240 --> 00:27:09,800
 Well more surfaces you bond to the more chance for that composite to peel away from those

308
00:27:09,800 --> 00:27:16,840
 walls when it shrinks which means more chance for micro leakage, more chance for post-operative

309
00:27:16,840 --> 00:27:18,640
 sensitivity.

310
00:27:18,640 --> 00:27:20,480
 So what do we do about it?

311
00:27:20,480 --> 00:27:25,680
 Well we can use smaller increments use two millimeter increments of composite and cure

312
00:27:25,680 --> 00:27:31,360
 each one before going on to the next increment.

313
00:27:31,360 --> 00:27:37,200
 So there's one last thing we need to cover in this video and that is the glass ionomers.

314
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:43,520
 Though it's the other side of the spectrum of the tooth colored restorations.

315
00:27:43,520 --> 00:27:50,800
 So salt matrix or glass ionomer came from the UK and resin matrix or composite resin

316
00:27:50,800 --> 00:27:58,440
 came from the US and both originated interestingly enough in the second half of the 20th century.

317
00:27:58,440 --> 00:28:04,520
 Now we've already talked at length about composite resin so you know all of this already and

318
00:28:04,520 --> 00:28:12,720
 while resins contain a matrix and a filler glass ionomers consist of an acid and a base

319
00:28:12,720 --> 00:28:22,560
 specifically polyacrylic acid and fluoroaluminosilicate glass or FAS glass and these are particularly

320
00:28:22,560 --> 00:28:29,720
 polyacrylic acid are really important to know when they come up on the board exam quite frequently.

321
00:28:29,720 --> 00:28:32,680
 So advantages of glass ionomers.

322
00:28:32,680 --> 00:28:39,040
 Self adhesion to enamel and dentin which means that you don't need etching or priming or

323
00:28:39,040 --> 00:28:46,280
 bonding you rely instead on chemical bonding between the carboxylic groups in the glass

324
00:28:46,280 --> 00:28:49,280
 ionomer and the hydroxyapatite.

325
00:28:49,280 --> 00:28:56,200
 This chemical bond is known as calcium chelation and you also get the benefits of fluoride storage

326
00:28:56,200 --> 00:29:01,520
 and release within the material and less overall shrinkage.

327
00:29:01,520 --> 00:29:05,080
 But the glass ionomers also come with limitations.

328
00:29:05,080 --> 00:29:12,040
 It's a weaker material has a longer setting time with a lack of control over when exactly

329
00:29:12,040 --> 00:29:14,040
 it sets.

330
00:29:14,040 --> 00:29:20,800
 So during the 1990s a major development was hybridization of the technology of these two

331
00:29:20,800 --> 00:29:27,640
 in an attempt to develop materials that exhibit the best characteristics of each.

332
00:29:27,640 --> 00:29:33,640
 So the various hybrids fall on a spectrum between the salt matrix glass ionomers and

333
00:29:33,640 --> 00:29:38,020
 the resin matrix composite resins.

334
00:29:38,020 --> 00:29:43,400
 So first we have resin modified glass ionomers and they fall here on the spectrum closer to

335
00:29:43,400 --> 00:29:47,160
 glass ionomers than to resin.

336
00:29:47,160 --> 00:29:53,400
 Because it's a hybrid it sets by both an acid based reaction from the glass ionomer component

337
00:29:53,400 --> 00:29:59,680
 and free radical addition polymerization which is from the resin component.

338
00:29:59,680 --> 00:30:05,760
 We have more rapid polymerization thanks to the free radical initiation of the resin

339
00:30:05,760 --> 00:30:12,160
 component and you gain some control over the setting time if it's light cure.

340
00:30:12,160 --> 00:30:17,380
 You also get some fluoride release and it's stronger than the conventional glass ionomer

341
00:30:17,380 --> 00:30:23,680
 so you get some unique benefits mixing these two in this way.

342
00:30:23,680 --> 00:30:27,360
 Next if we move a little bit more to the right on the spectrum we get the compramers

343
00:30:27,360 --> 00:30:31,360
 or poly acid modified resin composites.

344
00:30:31,360 --> 00:30:36,960
 These are anhydrous single paste that contain major ingredients of both composite resins

345
00:30:36,960 --> 00:30:42,240
 and glass ionomers except for water which is what anhydrous means.

346
00:30:42,240 --> 00:30:48,200
 The exclusion of water ensures that the initial setting occurs only by polymerization and

347
00:30:48,200 --> 00:30:53,160
 prevents premature setting so you have a little bit more time to work with it as long as you

348
00:30:53,160 --> 00:30:56,480
 keep the area dry.

349
00:30:56,480 --> 00:31:01,360
 And again that acid based reaction of the glass ionomer component may occur later as

350
00:31:01,360 --> 00:31:06,840
 the material absorbs water from the oral cavity hence why you want to keep the area dry while

351
00:31:06,840 --> 00:31:08,480
 you're working with it.

352
00:31:08,480 --> 00:31:13,520
 It's attracted perhaps the most attention in orthodontics for cementing bands because

353
00:31:13,520 --> 00:31:18,560
 the slower polymerization allows for more time to clean up excess around those bands

354
00:31:18,560 --> 00:31:25,040
 and you gain potentially although nominal some fluoride release benefit.

355
00:31:25,040 --> 00:31:30,920
 And lastly we have the ionomer modified composite which is essentially the exact opposite of

356
00:31:30,920 --> 00:31:36,160
 the resin modified glass ionomer just switching the words around so it's closer to the other

357
00:31:36,160 --> 00:31:38,400
 end of the spectrum.

358
00:31:38,400 --> 00:31:44,080
 This one behaves mostly like composites but contains some of those ion leachable classes

359
00:31:44,080 --> 00:31:49,080
 in an attempt to gain some very minimal fluoride release.

360
00:31:49,080 --> 00:31:54,480
 This one's perhaps the least common of the hybrids.

